<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antiochus I Theos of Commagene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 70–38 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontid-dynasty">Orontid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antiochus I Theos was king of Commagene from about 70 to 38 BCE. Claiming descent from both the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, he created a royal cult that merged Greek and Persian gods with himself, and built the great tomb sanctuary on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut">Mount Nemrut</a>. He ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> as a client of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which recognised him after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s eastern campaigns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antiochus-i-theos-of-commagene.jpg" alt="Antiochus I Theos of Commagene" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Verity Cridland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nemrut_Dag_area_A_Commagene_carving.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Antiochus I was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-callinicus">Mithridates I Callinicus</a> and Queen Laodice VII Thea of Commagene. His mother was a Seleucid princess, daughter of Antiochus VIII Grypus and the Ptolemaic princess Tryphaena, which made him a descendant of several of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a>, the generals who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. His father was possibly related to the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> and claimed descent from Orontes and, through Orontes' marriage to Rhodogune, daughter of Artaxerxes II, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><p>Little is known of his early life apart from its philhellenic character; he seems to have succeeded his father in 70 BCE. He married Isias, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariobarzanes-i-of-cappadocia">Ariobarzanes I of Cappadocia</a>, and they had at least four children, among them Mithridates II, who succeeded him, and Laodice, who married Orodes II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Commagene was a small kingdom in the highlands of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, bordering Cappadocia to the north and Osroene to the south. It had been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> and was considerably Hellenized, but in the 1st century BCE Iranian culture revived there, supported to stress the kingdom's ancient ancestry against Seleucid, Parthian and Roman claims. Antiochus suffered in the Roman war with Pontus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Armenia at first extended its influence over Commagene, but he was forced to side with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> declared war against him. His inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut">Mount Nemrut</a> calls him a friend of the Romans, though some of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s informants distrusted him.</p><p>He allied with the Parthian king Orodes II, and in 51 BCE nevertheless gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> intelligence on a Parthian force under Prince Pacorus I. In 38 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> defeated and killed Pacorus, and the remnants of his army took refuge in Commagene. Publius Ventidius besieged the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> to punish Antiochus. He offered 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and a renewed alliance, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> refused; Antony then took over the siege, could not take the city, and accepted a new offer of 300 talents. Antiochus's later life is obscure, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> he was killed by the Parthian king Phraates IV.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Antiochus built the sanctuary on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut">Mount Nemrut</a>, creating a royal cult in which he expected to be worshipped after death. His inscriptions say his tomb should stand on a high and holy place close to the gods; these gods were a blend of Greek and Iranian figures, such as Zeus-Oromasdes and Apollo-Mithras-Helios-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a>, with Persian dress and Greek faces. Feasts on the 10th and 16th of each month marked his coronation and birthday, and families of priests were endowed to serve in perpetuity. In 1883 German archaeologists excavated the tomb, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed it in 1987. Reliefs there include some of the oldest known images of two figures shaking hands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antiochus I of Commagene” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Antiochus I Theos</b> ruled from Samosata and devised a royal cult in which Greek and Persian gods were merged.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-commagene">Kingdom of Commagene</a></li></ul></section>
